# Models

`doc/models/` is the project's design layer: the [[model-lenses|lenses]] an
architect writes to declare *what* the system is and *how it must behave*, before
and alongside the [[implementation]] under `src/`. This card is the index — it
names every facet folder and says what belongs in each and how they feed one
another.

A model is not one document; it is a **stack of lenses**, each a focused,
implementation-free [[blueprint]] of one facet at the depth that facet needs. See
[[model-lenses]] for what a lens is (a format, not a folder) and [[len]] for the
notation and its L0/L1 levels.

## The facets

`doc/models/` is sorted by facet, not by format. The standing subfolders, in the
order design usually flows:

- `doc/models/requirements/` — **what must be true** before anything is designed:
  captured requirements, constraints, acceptance criteria, and the stakeholder
  intent behind them. This is the most generic and most basic step of system
  design; everything downstream references it.
- `doc/models/domain/` — **the concepts**: [[domain-driven-design]] models,
  bounded contexts, ontologies, and [[uml]] class/structure diagrams naming the
  things the system reasons about and their relations.
- `doc/models/data/` — **the structures**: database schemas,
  entity-relationship models, and [[data-modeling]] of what the system stores and
  moves.
- `doc/models/behavior/` — **the behavior**: algorithms, state machines, sequence
  and activity diagrams, and executable-intent specs from
  [[test-driven-development]] and [[behavior-driven-development]].

The set is open-ended, but these four cover the classical design artifacts.

## Requirements come first

Requirement capture is not paperwork — it is **system-design engineering**, and
it is often the decisive point of whether a system flies in production. It
answers the prior question to all the others: *what are we actually building, and
what must hold for it to count as done?* Because it is so foundational, it earns
its own facet: a requirement written once, in one place, can be **referenced
across the rest of the documentation body** rather than restated and drifting.

Well-captured requirements are what later
[proposals](../../proposals/README.md) reason from: an ADR/RFC records a
*decision*, and a decision is only as sound as the requirement it serves.
A requirement lens is therefore the natural [`**Source**`](../../../.grem/styles/doc/adr/prompt.md)
an ADR links back to — capture drives design, design drives the record.

## How the facets relate

The flow is a chain of lenses, each grounding the next:

```text
requirements  ->  domain + data  ->  behavior  ->  proposals (ADRs)  ->  src/ + tests/
   what must         the concepts      how it        the decisions       the code and
   be true           and structures    behaves       that satisfy them   its proofs
```

Each arrow is a link, not a copy: a domain lens cites the requirements it
realizes, a behavior lens cites the domain and data it acts on, and an
[[test-driven-development]] behavior lens is where obligations become tests. Keep
the layers cross-linked so the design is discoverable in both directions.

## Formats and styles

Any facet may be written in whichever notation pins it down — formal [[len]], or
hyper-markdown (`.hmd`) prose with diagrams (see [[model-lenses]]). Two shipped
documentation styles help produce them: [`doc/lenses`](../../../.grem/styles/doc/lenses/prompt.md)
turns a source into an L0/L1 lens under a facet, and
[`doc/hmd`](../../../.grem/styles/doc/hmd/prompt.md) writes the generic wiki-card
format used across `doc/wiki/`.

See [[grem-cli]] for how grem stamps `doc/models/`.
